  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nRadar trends at the time of writing depicts several clusters of\nthunderstorm activity from the Midwest into the Appalachians.\nSeveral rounds of severe and flooding have already pressed\nthrough the forecast area today, contributing to widespread tree\nand power line damage, as well as life-threatening flash \nflooding. \n\nAtmospheric recovery efforts already appears underway, with the\nwestern flank of the forecast area branching back out into\nsunshine and temperatures quickly rising back into the 70s to\nalmost low 80s. Focusing on convection upstream, a corridor of\nsevere strength storms stretches from central Indiana into the\nColumbus area of Ohio and tracking south-southeastward. Storms \nwill likely follow the path of least resistance when it comes to\ntheir propagation through the Ohio Valley, and that will be \nstrongly based on areas of best destabilization. With that in\nmind, opted to hoist a Severe Thunderstorm Watch for parts of\nKentucky and Ohio through the afternoon as we closely track this\nline. As has been the case with the history of storms today,\nconvective activity will be capable of producing damaging to\ndestructive wind gusts, quarter size hail, embedded rotation\nthat could touch down into a tornado, and heavy downpours.\n\nConfidence in the forecast for the overnight period remains low,\nwith each cycle run of our readily Convective Available Models\nsuggesting additional development of storms along a pseudo-warm\nfront parked overhead. Repetition of these overnight storms will\ncontribute to ongoing flash flooding concerns across the\nforecast area. The Flood Watch remains in effect tonight into\nmost of the work week.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nDaily chances for showers and thunderstorms triumphs through at\nleast Thursday while a frontal boundary remains draped through\nthe heart of the Ohio Valley. Propagation of these storms will\nremain closely tied to the low to mid level wind field each day,\nwhich will sometimes cause a southeastward approach of\nconvection and at other times bypass our forecast area\naltogether. Complexes that are able to hold their composure and\nventure into the area will bring damaging wind gusts and \ntorrential heavy rain. Sufficient rainfall producers will only\nexacerbate already inundated soils more and could lead to easily\nachievable flash flooding. Thus, the Flood Watch remains in \nplace through Friday morning. \n\nThe start of the weekend trends slightly drier with the mass\nexodus of the stalled front, but may quickly fill back in with\nadditional convection aiming for the Central Appalachians for\nSunday and onward.",
